01:27:36.250 --> 01:27:39.160
But as I got to use the tool
more, I was like wow, you know,

01:27:39.160 --> 01:27:42.350
this really can do
some pretty cool stuff.

01:27:42.350 --> 01:27:45.040
I was able to develop a
relatively sophisticated app

01:27:45.040 --> 01:27:47.750
for login monitoring
from ideation

01:27:47.750 --> 01:27:50.410
all the way through
the final delivered app

01:27:50.410 --> 01:27:53.497
to our administrators from
them to use was five weeks.

01:27:53.497 --> 01:27:55.330
And that's just unheard
of in any other kind

01:27:55.330 --> 01:27:57.550
of product lifecycle.

01:27:57.550 --> 01:27:59.080
- Why App Maker?

01:27:59.080 --> 01:28:02.500
Because not everyone is me.

01:28:02.500 --> 01:28:06.490
I really like working deep down
in the code working low level.

01:28:06.490 --> 01:28:08.500
The great thing
about App Maker is,

01:28:08.500 --> 01:28:11.140
you don't need to have
that deep-down knowledge.

01:28:11.140 --> 01:28:14.020
People are able to make
these really snazzy

01:28:14.020 --> 01:28:18.100
looking applications that can
be used by an entire company.

01:28:18.100 --> 01:28:20.670
- I never pictured myself
developing anything.

01:28:20.670 --> 01:28:26.300
App Maker is an insanely awesome
way to solve a small problem.

01:28:26.300 --> 01:28:29.200
- App Maker makes my
job easier because I

01:28:29.200 --> 01:28:31.320
don't need multiple tools.

01:28:31.320 --> 01:28:36.010
I can just develop it
directly on App Maker.

01:28:36.010 --> 01:28:38.770
- One word to describe
App Maker, simple.

01:28:38.770 --> 01:28:39.400
- Quick.

01:28:39.400 --> 01:28:40.000
- Flexible.

01:28:40.000 --> 01:28:40.499
- Versatile.

01:28:40.499 --> 01:28:41.470
- Empowerment.

01:28:41.470 --> 01:28:44.600
- One word to describe
App Maker, game changer.

01:28:49.777 --> 01:28:50.360
[END PLAYBACK]

01:28:50.360 --> 01:28:58.390
DARYOUSH GOLJAHANI:
[SPEAKING ITALIAN]

01:29:22.370 --> 01:29:26.290
[APPLAUSE]

01:29:32.680 --> 01:29:34.322
MILES WARD: Good
morning, everybody.

01:29:34.322 --> 01:29:36.530
I'm one of the ones that's
going to speak in English.

01:29:36.530 --> 01:29:39.140
So I'll apologize in advance.

01:29:39.140 --> 01:29:40.130
My name's Miles Ward.

01:29:40.130 --> 01:29:42.470
I run our solutions
architect team.

01:29:42.470 --> 01:29:44.720
And what I'm here
to talk to you today

01:29:44.720 --> 01:29:48.680
is about how we were able to
make all of the innovations

01:29:48.680 --> 01:29:49.970
you just heard about.

01:29:49.970 --> 01:29:53.330
How did we build a system that
was this intelligent, that

01:29:53.330 --> 01:29:55.850
allows others to
collaborate, that

01:29:55.850 --> 01:29:59.570
creates such a powerful
platform for your data

01:29:59.570 --> 01:30:01.520
and for your applications?

01:30:01.520 --> 01:30:05.060
The reason we're able to do
that is because we thought first

01:30:05.060 --> 01:30:07.010
about openness.

01:30:07.010 --> 01:30:08.330
That may seem weird.

01:30:08.330 --> 01:30:10.490
Isn't Google full
of computer nerds

01:30:10.490 --> 01:30:13.820
that are going to stay up late
and build great technology?

01:30:13.820 --> 01:30:17.930
Well, we've been learning from
computer nerds for a long time.

01:30:17.930 --> 01:30:20.120
And what the very best
of us have realized,

01:30:20.120 --> 01:30:23.850
even long ago, is that
an approach that is open,

01:30:23.850 --> 01:30:26.750
that's flexible, that listens
to our customers, that

01:30:26.750 --> 01:30:30.410
listens to our partners, and
really builds exactly what they

01:30:30.410 --> 01:30:32.550
need, is the right way forward.

01:30:32.550 --> 01:30:35.160
And we've been doing
that for a long time.

01:30:35.160 --> 01:30:37.284
How many folks use the internet?

01:30:37.284 --> 01:30:38.230
Yeah?

01:30:38.230 --> 01:30:40.630
Two or three folks here?

01:30:40.630 --> 01:30:44.080
The internet was designed
open, built by Vint Cerf--

01:30:44.080 --> 01:30:46.870
he's a Google
employee, by the way--

01:30:46.870 --> 01:30:49.420
who designed the
protocol that we all

01:30:49.420 --> 01:30:52.210
use to connect all
of the businesses

01:30:52.210 --> 01:30:54.490
and all the technologies
in the world.

01:30:54.490 --> 01:30:56.200
That commitment to
open technology--

01:30:56.200 --> 01:31:00.190
they took no patents, no
copyrights, no licenses--

01:31:00.190 --> 01:31:04.270
allowed billions and
billions of dollars

01:31:04.270 --> 01:31:07.420
of international innovation
that stood on top

01:31:07.420 --> 01:31:09.490
of something that's free.

01:31:09.490 --> 01:31:11.800
And Google's thought
about the investments

01:31:11.800 --> 01:31:14.920
that your business needs
to make and how important

01:31:14.920 --> 01:31:18.610
it is that those investments
pay dividends over time, instead

01:31:18.610 --> 01:31:22.300
of being something that you pay
for and pay for and pay for.

01:31:22.300 --> 01:31:25.960
So Google's made a really
substantial commitment

01:31:25.960 --> 01:31:28.390
and investment in open
source technologies.

01:31:28.390 --> 01:31:31.810
Almost all of the tools
that you saw today

01:31:31.810 --> 01:31:36.970
bear fruit from our investment,
so from work in Linux and core

01:31:36.970 --> 01:31:39.730
compilers, 10
years of investment

01:31:39.730 --> 01:31:44.090
by Google engineers in
software that's free

01:31:44.090 --> 01:31:47.010
and as a leader in the
open source community,

01:31:47.010 --> 01:31:49.880
it's not just a one-time effort.

01:31:49.880 --> 01:31:53.960
Our engineers have to work
overtime, all the time,

01:31:53.960 --> 01:31:56.060
to make it so that these
technologies continue

01:31:56.060 --> 01:32:01.010
to become more performant, more
robust, more highly available,

01:32:01.010 --> 01:32:05.120
more secure, easier, simpler.

01:32:05.120 --> 01:32:10.040
We put 15,000 new projects into
collaboration just in 2016.

01:32:10.040 --> 01:32:13.430
You get 2,500 different
projects where we're

01:32:13.430 --> 01:32:15.440
contributing source code today.

01:32:15.440 --> 01:32:18.080
That's the majority
of the available tools

01:32:18.080 --> 01:32:19.640
that are out there.

01:32:19.640 --> 01:32:21.800
If you think about maybe
one of the most important

01:32:21.800 --> 01:32:24.320
contributions in
the last 15 years

01:32:24.320 --> 01:32:27.600
has been our donation
of the cgroup code

01:32:27.600 --> 01:32:29.540
to the Linux kernel.

01:32:29.540 --> 01:32:32.550
cgroups is what now the
world calls containers.

01:32:32.550 --> 01:32:34.297
How many folks have
heard of containers?

01:32:34.297 --> 01:32:35.430
Yeah?

01:32:35.430 --> 01:32:36.200
OK.

01:32:36.200 --> 01:32:38.180
We thought that was a
good idea, because we

01:32:38.180 --> 01:32:42.410
saw what a huge impact that
had inside of our business.

01:32:42.410 --> 01:32:44.930
Every one of the products
that you saw today,

01:32:44.930 --> 01:32:48.890
all of Google's technology
is run inside of containers.

01:32:48.890 --> 01:32:51.230
And we know that if it
paid dividends for us

01:32:51.230 --> 01:32:53.780
to make that change, that
it's very likely that it

01:32:53.780 --> 01:32:57.020
will have an incredible
impact on your businesses.

01:32:57.020 --> 01:32:59.960
And that's not just a commitment
to building technology

01:32:59.960 --> 01:33:00.620
that's good.

01:33:00.620 --> 01:33:02.420
And Kubernetes is really good.

01:33:02.420 --> 01:33:04.100
It allows you to
build systems that

01:33:04.100 --> 01:33:06.980
are highly available,
that are redundant,

01:33:06.980 --> 01:33:09.920
and maybe
counterintuitively, something

01:33:09.920 --> 01:33:11.900
that can run on any cloud.

01:33:11.900 --> 01:33:14.480
I'm more than happy to see
you build an application which

01:33:14.480 --> 01:33:17.510
spreads across us
and our competitors

01:33:17.510 --> 01:33:19.940
and your laptop
and my mobile phone

01:33:19.940 --> 01:33:22.550
and everywhere else you
want to build an app.

01:33:22.550 --> 01:33:26.600
Kubernetes has the flexibility
and the openness to empower you

01:33:26.600 --> 01:33:28.910
wherever you want to build.

01:33:28.910 --> 01:33:32.600
That ensures that your
investment in this powerful API

01:33:32.600 --> 01:33:35.420
is one that pays
dividends over time.

01:33:35.420 --> 01:33:39.650
That community, the community
of you and us and our partners

01:33:39.650 --> 01:33:41.840
and our customers,
working together

01:33:41.840 --> 01:33:44.210
to improve this
technology starts

01:33:44.210 --> 01:33:47.930
to sound like an
incredibly fun party.

01:33:47.930 --> 01:33:52.100
You have Microsoft and IBM
and us all working together

01:33:52.100 --> 01:33:53.510
on the same technology?

01:33:53.510 --> 01:33:56.860
That's a different,
better future, I think.

01:33:56.860 --> 01:34:01.340
So we're also seeing this
technology and our commitment

01:34:01.340 --> 01:34:05.480
to open move through our
different technology tools.

01:34:05.480 --> 01:34:07.370
So App Engine
flexible-- how many

01:34:07.370 --> 01:34:10.010
folks develop in a language
that none of your friends

01:34:10.010 --> 01:34:11.420
have heard of?

01:34:11.420 --> 01:34:14.120
You're really good
at Scala or Erling.

01:34:14.120 --> 01:34:16.820
Or you want to write
everything in COBOL,

01:34:16.820 --> 01:34:18.350
because you work at a bank.

01:34:18.350 --> 01:34:20.450
We have an environment
that allows

01:34:20.450 --> 01:34:23.270
you to take even that
most exotic software

01:34:23.270 --> 01:34:26.000
and run it in a managed
service from Google.

01:34:26.000 --> 01:34:28.220
Because you can supply
your own runtime.

01:34:28.220 --> 01:34:30.920
That's an open
platform, instead of

01:34:30.920 --> 01:34:33.620
one that forces you
down a narrow path.

01:34:33.620 --> 01:34:35.150
Google wants to
continue to give you

01:34:35.150 --> 01:34:38.540
proprietary performance,
incredible security,

01:34:38.540 --> 01:34:43.220
incredible speed, but on the
technology that you choose.

01:34:43.220 --> 01:34:45.650
And that's not just open source.

01:34:45.650 --> 01:34:47.870
That's technology from
any vendor, right?

01:34:47.870 --> 01:34:50.150
We've made incredible
progress in our partnerships

01:34:50.150 --> 01:34:51.854
with Microsoft.

01:34:51.854 --> 01:34:54.020
I don't know if you've heard
of Perfkit Benchmarker,

01:34:54.020 --> 01:34:57.320
but I recommend everybody
take a time to download this

01:34:57.320 --> 01:35:00.260
and look at the performance
benchmarks of running Windows

01:35:00.260 --> 01:35:02.930
software on Google's cloud.

01:35:02.930 --> 01:35:05.120
I think you'll come
away surprised.

01:35:05.120 --> 01:35:07.580
Our environment is
not just an OK place

01:35:07.580 --> 01:35:09.650
to run Windows applications.

01:35:09.650 --> 01:35:11.180
It's an incredible place.

01:35:11.180 --> 01:35:13.010
We have the highest
performance network,

01:35:13.010 --> 01:35:16.220
the most high performance
storage, and advanced machines.

01:35:16.220 --> 01:35:18.620
We just released
computers on Skylake.

01:35:18.620 --> 01:35:22.610
Those systems allow you to run
Visual Studio and SQL Server

01:35:22.610 --> 01:35:26.210
and all of the broad
suite of Microsoft tools

01:35:26.210 --> 01:35:29.000
as if this is their
original home.

01:35:29.000 --> 01:35:33.050
And it's not just the thin,
underlying pieces, not just

01:35:33.050 --> 01:35:34.070
Windows.

01:35:34.070 --> 01:35:38.510
You can run SQL Server
Enterprise 2012 or 2014, 2016.

01:35:38.510 --> 01:35:42.950
You can use full .NET Core, all
of the rich tooling you expect,

01:35:42.950 --> 01:35:45.630
lots of modules
built for PowerShell,

01:35:45.630 --> 01:35:50.180
an incredible rich ecosystem
around the Windows platform.

01:35:50.180 --> 01:35:52.100
We also talk to
customers, and what

01:35:52.100 --> 01:35:56.210
they told us was that all of the
technology seems very flexible,

01:35:56.210 --> 01:35:57.560
but are the people flexible?

01:35:57.560 --> 01:35:59.360
I'm not very flexible.

01:35:59.360 --> 01:36:02.000
I don't have very
good dance moves.

01:36:02.000 --> 01:36:05.600
But our customers
said, I need support

01:36:05.600 --> 01:36:09.735
to feel as flexible
as your computers do.

01:36:09.735 --> 01:36:11.360
So we started to
think about our model.

01:36:11.360 --> 01:36:14.030
Most companies will charge you
a percentage of how much you

01:36:14.030 --> 01:36:16.189
spend on cloud for support.

01:36:16.189 --> 01:36:18.230
And at the beginning, that
seems like not enough.

01:36:18.230 --> 01:36:21.680
I pay you $5 for
incredible engineers?

01:36:21.680 --> 01:36:23.330
And at the end,
when you've built

01:36:23.330 --> 01:36:25.010
a system that works
right, you end up

01:36:25.010 --> 01:36:27.435
paying this kind of success tax.

01:36:27.435 --> 01:36:29.940
And we knew our engineers
wouldn't like that,

01:36:29.940 --> 01:36:31.880
and so we know
yours won't either.

01:36:31.880 --> 01:36:34.040
Our new model for
enterprise support,

01:36:34.040 --> 01:36:37.220
which is engineer to
engineer, real Google dorks,

01:36:37.220 --> 01:36:41.150
just like me, on the phone with
you, trying to fix your app,

01:36:41.150 --> 01:36:43.340
where you pay just for
the number of folks

01:36:43.340 --> 01:36:45.770
on your team that
can talk to us.

01:36:45.770 --> 01:36:48.259
So support can auto scale.

01:36:48.259 --> 01:36:50.300
It's a totally different
model than anywhere else

01:36:50.300 --> 01:36:50.966
in the industry.

01:36:50.966 --> 01:36:54.140
And we know that it is much
better for our businesses

01:36:54.140 --> 01:36:55.250
and our partners.

01:36:55.250 --> 01:36:57.960
We've also heard from
customers that pricing

01:36:57.960 --> 01:36:59.240
isn't very straightforward.

01:36:59.240 --> 01:37:00.380
Pricing is complex.

01:37:00.380 --> 01:37:03.810
How many folks think the
cloud bills are easy?

01:37:03.810 --> 01:37:06.320
Note no hands are up.

01:37:06.320 --> 01:37:10.000
So we heard from customers
that the cost of cloud

01:37:10.000 --> 01:37:11.640
can be incredible.

01:37:11.640 --> 01:37:13.370
And a lot of that
comes from waste.

01:37:13.370 --> 01:37:17.600
In other environments, you pay
per hour instead of per minute.

01:37:17.600 --> 01:37:20.600
In other environments you
have to commit capital upfront

01:37:20.600 --> 01:37:23.060
instead of automatically
growing your services.

01:37:23.060 --> 01:37:25.550
You have all of
this inflexibility

01:37:25.550 --> 01:37:27.800
in what was supposed to be
cloud, what was supposed

01:37:27.800 --> 01:37:29.720
to be incredibly flexible.

01:37:29.720 --> 01:37:32.660
So we've made it so
that any time you

01:37:32.660 --> 01:37:35.390
run a computer longer
than 25% of the month,

01:37:35.390 --> 01:37:37.130
you automatically
get a discount.

01:37:37.130 --> 01:37:39.170
You cannot screw that up.

01:37:39.170 --> 01:37:43.092
You can't accidentally
not get the discount.

01:37:43.092 --> 01:37:45.050
We've also made it so
that our virtual machines

01:37:45.050 --> 01:37:46.220
can be any size.

01:37:46.220 --> 01:37:48.530
Do you want a virtual
machine that's

01:37:48.530 --> 01:37:51.950
18 cores and 68.25 gigs of RAM?

01:37:51.950 --> 01:37:53.150
That's fine.

01:37:53.150 --> 01:37:55.580
Whatever you need and what's
most efficient for you,

01:37:55.580 --> 01:37:57.480
we can provide at
a low, low price.

01:37:57.480 --> 01:37:59.450
That's because we're
built on containers.

01:37:59.450 --> 01:38:01.247
We're just a little
more flexible.

01:38:01.247 --> 01:38:03.830
If you don't know what the right
size for your application is,

01:38:03.830 --> 01:38:05.000
that's OK, too.

01:38:05.000 --> 01:38:06.260
We will tell you.

01:38:06.260 --> 01:38:07.880
And we actually
give you a button,

01:38:07.880 --> 01:38:10.430
so that you can automatically
resize your instance

01:38:10.430 --> 01:38:11.540
to the right size.

01:38:11.540 --> 01:38:13.984
Can it get easier than that?

01:38:13.984 --> 01:38:15.650
We've also made it
so that these systems

01:38:15.650 --> 01:38:16.920
are available per minute.

01:38:16.920 --> 01:38:19.370
So even if you need an
auto scaling application

01:38:19.370 --> 01:38:21.470
to process data
for 12 minutes, you

01:38:21.470 --> 01:38:26.600
don't waste 5/6 of the money.

01:38:26.600 --> 01:38:29.690
If you know for sure how
your application works,

01:38:29.690 --> 01:38:31.460
if you have a commitment
with a customer

01:38:31.460 --> 01:38:34.070
to run an application
for years, you

01:38:34.070 --> 01:38:36.560
should be able to take advantage
of that prior knowledge.

01:38:36.560 --> 01:38:38.300
And we need the same data.

01:38:38.300 --> 01:38:40.130
So committed use
discounts allows

01:38:40.130 --> 01:38:43.340
you to pay upfront
simply just for bucket

01:38:43.340 --> 01:38:45.320
full of cores and RAM.

01:38:45.320 --> 01:38:48.076
No specific instances,
no specific zones--

01:38:48.076 --> 01:38:49.700
you pick exactly what
you need from us,

01:38:49.700 --> 01:38:52.280
and we'll give you
up to a 57% discount.

01:38:52.280 --> 01:38:58.180
And different than the grocery
store, these discounts stack.

01:38:58.180 --> 01:39:00.370
So in total against
our competitors,

01:39:00.370 --> 01:39:04.120
who are already dramatically
more price-efficient than doing

01:39:04.120 --> 01:39:09.190
this in your own data centers,
we're another 60% cheaper.

01:39:09.190 --> 01:39:10.990
And that's on average.

01:39:10.990 --> 01:39:13.150
I've talked to several
developers here in Italy

01:39:13.150 --> 01:39:17.110
already, and I've met nobody
that I would consider average.

01:39:17.110 --> 01:39:19.750
I think you can save
even more than this.

01:39:19.750 --> 01:39:24.190
So I'm really excited
about your contributions

01:39:24.190 --> 01:39:26.600
to open source, to a
flexible environment,

01:39:26.600 --> 01:39:28.552
to building great
services for customers.

01:39:28.552 --> 01:39:30.760
And I've talked to some
incredible customers already.

01:39:30.760 --> 01:39:33.190
We're going to bring a
whole set of customers

01:39:33.190 --> 01:39:34.400
onstage to talk to you.

01:39:34.400 --> 01:39:36.870
Thank you so much
for your time today.

